---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Treadmill vs Elliptical (2026): Which One Should You Actually Buy?";
const description =
  "The elliptical is genuinely lower impact and the treadmill genuinely trains a skill you use. Compared on joints, calorie burn, bone density, footprint, noise and long-term cost.";

const COMPARE = [
  {
    factor: "Joint impact",
    treadmill: "Ground reaction force 2 – 3x body weight when running",
    elliptical: "Feet never leave the pedals. Near zero impact.",
  },
  {
    factor: "Calorie burn per hour",
    treadmill: "Higher at running paces; comparable at walking",
    elliptical: "Similar to a brisk walk; effort is easy to under-report",
  },
  {
    factor: "Bone density benefit",
    treadmill: "Weight-bearing with impact loading",
    elliptical: "Weight-bearing but minimal impact stimulus",
  },
  {
    factor: "Specificity for runners",
    treadmill: "Trains the actual movement and its mechanics",
    elliptical: "Cross-training only. Gait is not running.",
  },
  {
    factor: "Footprint",
    treadmill: "80 x 36 in typical, plus 6 ft clearance behind",
    elliptical: "70 x 28 in typical, but needs ceiling height",
  },
  {
    factor: "Noise to the room below",
    treadmill: "Impact noise transmits through structure",
    elliptical: "Quieter downstairs. A real advantage in apartments.",
  },
  {
    factor: "Maintenance",
    treadmill: "Deck lubrication, belt tension, belt replacement",
    elliptical: "Minimal. Occasional bolt check and pivot service.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill vs Elliptical", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a treadmill or an elliptical better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Neither is better in general — they suit different people. Buy a treadmill if you want to train walking or running, want the bone-loading benefit of impact, or are preparing for outdoor events. Buy an elliptical if joint pain limits what you can do, if impact noise to a downstairs neighbour is a real constraint, or if you want the lowest-maintenance option.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which burns more calories, a treadmill or an elliptical?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At equal effort the difference is small, but a treadmill makes higher effort easier to reach and harder to fake. Running at pace burns more per hour than most people achieve on an elliptical, because the elliptical's smooth motion and hand supports make it easy to coast without noticing. Console estimates on both machines run optimistic.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is an elliptical better for bad knees?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually yes. Your feet stay in contact with the pedals throughout, so the impact loading of running is absent entirely. That said, walking on a treadmill is also low impact and some knee conditions are aggravated by the elliptical's fixed stride path. Try both if you can, and take a clinician's advice over a general rule.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does an elliptical build bone density like running?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not to the same degree. Bone responds to impact loading and to sudden changes in force, which is exactly what an elliptical is designed to remove. It remains weight-bearing exercise and is far better than nothing, but if maintaining bone density is a specific goal, walking or running on a treadmill provides a stronger stimulus.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which takes up less space, a treadmill or an elliptical?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "An elliptical usually has a smaller floor footprint and no need for the 6 feet of safety clearance a treadmill wants behind it. The trade-off is height: an elliptical raises you 8 to 20 inches on the pedals and you rise further on each stride, so ceiling height often decides the question in a basement.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use an elliptical to train for a running race?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "As cross-training and for maintaining aerobic fitness through injury, yes, and it is one of the better options for that. As a substitute for running mileage, no. The gait is not running, the muscle recruitment differs, and the connective tissue adaptations that make running feel easy only come from running.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-06",
      dateModified: "2026-09-06",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-06",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-06",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill vs elliptical",
      "elliptical trainer",
      "low impact cardio",
      "home gym",
      "cardio equipment",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-ellip" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-ellip)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Treadmill vs <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Elliptical</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The elliptical genuinely removes impact. The treadmill genuinely trains a
          movement you use every day. Almost every honest recommendation comes down
          to which of those two things matters more in your particular situation.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#comparison" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Comparison
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Which One for You
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 6, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">The short answer.</strong> Treadmill if you
          want to walk or run, want the bone-loading benefit of impact, or are
          training for anything outdoors. Elliptical if joint pain limits you, if a
          downstairs neighbour is a genuine constraint, or if you want the machine
          that needs almost no maintenance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Side by Side</a>
          <a href="#impact" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Impact and Joints</a>
          <a href="#calories" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Calorie Burn, Honestly</a>
          <a href="#bone" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Bone Density and Loading</a>
          <a href="#specificity" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Training a Skill You Use</a>
          <a href="#space" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Space, Noise and Neighbours</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Cost Over Ten Years</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Which One for You</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This comparison is usually written as though one machine wins. It does
          not. The elliptical exists because impact is a real cost of running, and
          the treadmill persists because impact is also where several of running's
          benefits come from. What follows is the honest version of both sides,
          including where each machine is worse than its marketing suggests.
        </p>

        <div id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Side by Side</h2>
          <p>
            Seven factors that actually differ between the two, before the detail.
            Figures are for mainstream home machines rather than commercial ones.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[720px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Factor</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Treadmill</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Elliptical</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              COMPARE.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.factor}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.treadmill}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.elliptical}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Notice that the two machines trade places depending on the row. There is
          no column that wins outright, which is why a recommendation only becomes
          useful once you know which two or three of these rows matter to you.
        </p>

        <div id="impact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Impact and Joints</h2>
          <p>
            The elliptical's central claim, and it is true. Your feet remain in
            contact with the pedals through the whole cycle, so there is no landing —
            and without a landing there is no impact spike.
          </p>
          <p>
            Running generates ground reaction forces in the region of two to three
            times body weight at each footstrike, delivered in a few hundredths of a
            second. For someone with knee osteoarthritis, a hip replacement, or a
            history of stress fractures, that repeated loading is the specific thing
            they need to avoid, and the elliptical removes it entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two honest qualifications go with that. First, <strong>walking is also
            low impact</strong>. Walking generates forces close to 1.2 times body
            weight, not three, so a treadmill used for walking is not the
            high-impact machine the comparison implies. If your intention is to walk,
            the impact argument for the elliptical largely evaporates.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, the elliptical imposes a <strong>fixed stride path</strong>. Your
            feet travel the same oval regardless of what your hips and knees would
            prefer, and for some people that constraint is itself uncomfortable — a
            complaint that shows up particularly among taller users on machines with
            a short stride length. Low impact is not the same as universally
            comfortable.
          </p>
          <p>
            If joint comfort is the deciding factor and walking is acceptable to you,
            it is worth considering a machine chosen for walking specifically, which
            is what our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill testing</a>
            covers, before assuming the elliptical is the only low-impact option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Calorie Burn, Honestly</h2>
          <p>
            The most-searched question in this comparison and the least useful one,
            because the answer depends far more on how hard you work than on which
            machine you stand on.
          </p>
          <p>
            At equal effort, the two are closer than most claims suggest. What
            separates them in practice is that a treadmill makes high effort easier
            to reach and much harder to avoid. You cannot coast on a moving belt — if
            the belt runs at 7 mph, you run at 7 mph. On an elliptical, momentum in
            the flywheel, the smooth motion and the hand supports make it very easy
            to reduce your own contribution without noticing.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why observed studies tend to find higher average burn on
            treadmills: not because the machine is more efficient at converting
            effort, but because the machine enforces effort. For someone who trains
            with discipline and watches heart rate, that difference narrows a long
            way.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treat both consoles' calorie figures with suspicion. Neither machine
            knows your body composition or your efficiency, and elliptical consoles
            in particular tend to be optimistic because they do not know how much of
            the work your arms are contributing versus how much you are leaning. A
            heart rate monitor is a considerably better guide than either display.
          </p>
          <p>
            The genuinely decisive factor is adherence. The machine you use four
            times a week burns more than the machine you use once, and that is
            usually a question of which one you find tolerable rather than which one
            scores better on paper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Bone Density and Loading</h2>
          <p>
            The argument that runs the other way, and the one most people are not
            aware of when they choose the low-impact option.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bone adapts to the loads placed on it, and the stimulus it responds to
            most strongly is impact — rapid changes in force rather than steady
            pressure. This is why weight-bearing impact exercise is routinely
            recommended for maintaining bone mineral density, particularly for
            post-menopausal women and older adults generally.
          </p>
          <p>
            The elliptical is weight-bearing, which is a genuine advantage over
            cycling or swimming. But it is specifically engineered to remove the
            impact component, and that component is the part bone responds to most.
            An elliptical is therefore a weaker stimulus for bone than walking or
            running on a treadmill, and considerably weaker than running.
          </p>
          <p>
            This does not make the elliptical a bad choice. For someone who cannot
            tolerate impact, the comparison that matters is against doing nothing,
            and the elliptical wins that comfortably. But if bone density is a
            specific goal — and for many people over 50 it should be — it belongs in
            the decision rather than being crowded out by the joint-comfort argument.
          </p>
          <p>
            The middle path most often overlooked is <strong>inclined walking on a
            treadmill</strong>: weight-bearing, modest impact, high effort, and
            gentle on the knees compared with running. It is a large part of why the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 protocol</a>
            has been as popular as it has, and it is a genuine third option in this
            comparison rather than a compromise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specificity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Training a Skill You Use</h2>
          <p>
            The treadmill's strongest argument, and it applies well beyond runners.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walking and running are movements you perform outside the gym. Training
            them trains the specific muscle recruitment, the connective tissue
            tolerance and the balance and gait patterns you use crossing a road or
            climbing stairs. The elliptical's motion, by design, is not one you
            perform anywhere else.
          </p>
          <p>
            For runners this is decisive. Elliptical work maintains aerobic fitness
            well and is one of the better cross-training options during injury, but
            it does not build the tendon and bone tolerance that lets you run further
            without breaking down. A runner who spends a winter on an elliptical
            returns to running with their cardiovascular fitness intact and their
            tissue tolerance diminished, which is when injuries happen.
          </p>
          <p>
            For older adults the same logic applies in a different register.
            Maintaining a confident, stable walking gait is one of the more valuable
            things exercise can preserve, and it is trained by walking. That is part
            of why our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">senior treadmill guide</a>
            scores machines on step-up height and handrail length rather than on
            programme count.
          </p>
          <p>
            The honest counterweight: if you dislike walking and running, this
            argument is worth little. Specificity only pays out if you actually do
            the training, and an elliptical you enjoy beats a treadmill that becomes
            a clothes rail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Space, Noise and Neighbours</h2>
          <p>
            Often the factor that actually decides it, and the one people leave until
            after they have chosen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor space</strong> favours the elliptical, but not for the
            obvious reason. Footprints are broadly similar — an elliptical is
            typically shorter and narrower — but the treadmill needs about 6 feet of
            clear floor behind it as a safety margin, because that is where you go if
            you come off the back. An elliptical needs no such zone. In a tight room
            that difference is larger than the footprints suggest, as our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            sets out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling height</strong> favours the treadmill in most rooms.
            Elliptical pedals sit 8 to 20 inches off the floor depending on the
            machine, and you rise further through each stride, so a low basement
            ceiling frequently rules them out. Measure at the highest point of your
            stride, not standing still.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise</strong> favours the elliptical decisively for anyone with
            a room below. Treadmill noise reaching a downstairs neighbour is
            primarily impact noise transmitted through the structure, and it is
            difficult to control — mats and machine choice help, but do not solve it.
            An elliptical produces almost none of that. If you are in a flat with
            neighbours below, this factor can reasonably outrank everything else in
            this article; our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment noise testing</a>
            measured how much difference the quietest treadmills actually make.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Moving it</strong> favours the treadmill, marginally. Many fold;
            almost no ellipticals do, and a non-folding elliptical is a permanent
            fixture in the room you put it in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Cost Over Ten Years</h2>
          <p>
            Purchase prices overlap almost completely at every tier, so the
            interesting differences are in what happens afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance</strong> clearly favours the elliptical. A treadmill
            needs its deck lubricated several times a year, its belt tension checked,
            and eventually a new belt — and neglecting the first of those is the most
            common reason home treadmills die early. An elliptical asks for a bolt
            check and occasional attention to the pivots. Over a decade that is a
            meaningful difference in both money and attention.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Failure modes</strong> also favour the elliptical. A treadmill's
            expensive parts are the motor and the controller board, both of which are
            heat-sensitive and both of which can cost a substantial fraction of a
            replacement machine. An elliptical's drivetrain is simpler, runs cooler,
            and has less to go wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Electricity</strong> favours the elliptical too, though the
            amounts are small enough not to matter — a treadmill's motor draws real
            power while most ellipticals only power the console and the resistance
            mechanism. Both are trivial next to household heating, as our
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">electricity guide</a>
            works through.
          </p>
          <p>
            The counterweight is <strong>resale and longevity</strong>. A
            well-maintained treadmill from a serious brand lasts a long time and has a
            functioning second-hand market. The realistic lifespans, and the
            repair-or-replace arithmetic for each major component, are in
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Which One for You</h2>
          <p>
            Rather than a winner, here is the reasoning applied to the situations
            people actually arrive with.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy a treadmill</strong> if you run, or want to; if you are
            training for anything outdoors; if bone density is a specific concern; if
            you want incline as a training variable; or if walking is your main
            intention and you have the floor space for the safety clearance. The
            walking case is stronger than most comparisons allow, because walking is
            not the high-impact activity the elliptical is designed to replace.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy an elliptical</strong> if joint pain genuinely limits what
            you can do; if you have neighbours below and impact noise is a real
            constraint; if you want the lowest-maintenance machine available; or if
            you have tried both and simply prefer it. That last reason is better than
            it sounds — adherence beats optimisation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider a third option</strong> if the room is small or the
            budget is tight. A walking pad occupies a fraction of the space of
            either, stores under furniture, and suits anyone whose honest use case is
            walking while working; the format comparison is in
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill</a>.
            Compact hybrid trainers such as the
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex Max Trainer</a>
            occupy a similar niche to an elliptical in less floor area.
          </p>
          <p>
            One piece of advice that applies whichever way you go: try both before
            committing if you possibly can. Twenty minutes on each at a gym or a
            showroom tells you more about which machine you will actually use than
            any specification comparison, and the machine you use is the one that
            works. If it turns out to be a treadmill, our
            <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">complete buying guide</a>
            covers what to look for next.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill or an elliptical better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better in general — they suit different people. Buy a
                treadmill if you want to train walking or running, want the
                bone-loading benefit of impact, or are preparing for outdoor events.
                Buy an elliptical if joint pain limits what you can do, if impact
                noise to a downstairs neighbour is a real constraint, or if you want
                the lowest-maintenance option.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which burns more calories, a treadmill or an elliptical?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At equal effort the difference is small, but a treadmill makes higher
                effort easier to reach and harder to fake. Running at pace burns more
                per hour than most people achieve on an elliptical, because the
                elliptical's smooth motion and hand supports make it easy to coast
                without noticing. Console estimates on both machines run optimistic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an elliptical better for bad knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. Your feet stay in contact with the pedals throughout, so
                the impact loading of running is absent entirely. That said, walking
                on a treadmill is also low impact and some knee conditions are
                aggravated by the elliptical's fixed stride path. Try both if you can,
                and take a clinician's advice over a general rule.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an elliptical build bone density like running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to the same degree. Bone responds to impact loading and to sudden
                changes in force, which is exactly what an elliptical is designed to
                remove. It remains weight-bearing exercise and is far better than
                nothing, but if maintaining bone density is a specific goal, walking
                or running on a treadmill provides a stronger stimulus.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which takes up less space, a treadmill or an elliptical?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An elliptical usually has a smaller floor footprint and no need for
                the 6 feet of safety clearance a treadmill wants behind it. The
                trade-off is height: an elliptical raises you 8 to 20 inches on the
                pedals and you rise further on each stride, so ceiling height often
                decides the question in a basement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use an elliptical to train for a running race?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As cross-training and for maintaining aerobic fitness through injury,
                yes, and it is one of the better options for that. As a substitute for
                running mileage, no. The gait is not running, the muscle recruitment
                differs, and the connective tissue adaptations that make running feel
                easy only come from running.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing Between Them</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these leads to a machine that is technically fine and wrong for the person who bought it.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a treadmill means high impact</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Running generates two to three times body weight per footstrike; walking generates about 1.2. If your intention is to walk, the impact argument for the elliptical largely disappears, and you keep the bone-loading and gait-training benefits. Decide what you will actually do on the machine before applying the impact comparison.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on console calorie figures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Neither machine knows your body composition or how much you are leaning on it, and elliptical displays are especially generous because they cannot see how much work your arms are doing. At equal effort the two are close. Use a heart rate monitor if you want a real number, and choose on other grounds.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the clearance and the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill needs 6 feet of clear floor behind it that no footprint comparison shows. An elliptical raises you 8 to 20 inches and further again through each stride, which rules it out of many basements. Measure both the floor and the height above your head before comparing anything else.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating an elliptical as replacement running mileage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It maintains aerobic fitness well and is one of the better options during injury, but it does not build tendon and bone tolerance. A runner who spends a winter on one comes back aerobically fit and structurally underprepared, which is precisely when injuries occur. Rebuild running volume gradually regardless of how fit you feel.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

